  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Lingering snow showers today. Slick mountain pass and high \n  elevation roadways possible through this morning.\n\n- A southwesterly system on Sunday will bring additional light snow\n  accumulations.\n\n- A warming and drying trend will arrive by the middle of next week.\n\nThe forecast for western Montana and north central Idaho features \nlingering snow showers this morning under northwest flow aloft, \nwith the potential for brief redevelopment this afternoon. \nForecast models traditionally end precipitation too quickly in \npatterns such as this, so lingering showers into the afternoon \nwould not be surprising. Expect isolated slick roads early today, \nparticularly over mountain passes and higher valleys near the \nContinental Divide. Fortunately, warm ground temperatures and \nnear-freezing air temperatures will help keep overall travel \nimpacts minimal.\n\nA new weather system arrives on Sunday from the southwest, bringing \nwarmer, moist air over the region's existing cold air (also known\nas a warm front) to produce additional light snow. Minor \naccumulations will primarily focus on higher terrain and \nsouthwest-facing slopes. Because most of this snow will fall \nduring the daytime, valley accumulations and related travel \nimpacts should remain limited.\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, high pressure will build across the \nregion,bringing drier and warmer conditions to the mountains. \nHowever, valley locations may experience temperature inversions \nthat trap cooler air near the surface, increasing the potential \nfor persistent fog and low clouds. Another period of wet and \nrelatively mild weather is possible late in the week (possibly as \nearly as Thanksgiving) as a moisture rich system moves toward the \nNorthern Rockies.",
